{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EgxNjsg7Par8jCAcBuCAsx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clearer picture. Even when I exclude `test_position_monitor_constraints.py` and `test_r78_trade_pnl_calculation.py`, there are still JSONB errors in other test files (`test_portfolio_manager.py`, `test_position_monitor.py`).\n\nIn total, without `api` and without those specific files:\n- 23 failed (these might be real failures, not JSONB-related)\n- 381 passed\n- 56 errors (JSONB)\n\nThe 23 failures - let me understand what they are.\n\n\nThe real issue is that when CI runs the pytest command with `--maxfail=10`, it's hitting JSONB errors in tests that hardcode SQLite while the CI environment has PostgreSQL, causing the job to fail before getting through all the tests.\n\nBut I need to figure out whether this is a new regression from the recent changes or if these tests were already broken when they were disabled. The key is understanding what state the codebase was in before these tests were re-enabled.\n\nLooking at the issue description, it claims that QuantAgent-sfc and QuantAgent-4ch resolved the problems, but the JSONB incompatibility issues with SQLite are clearly still present across multiple test files. So my assessment is that this is a partial pass—the workflow structure itself is correct, but the test suite will actually fail in CI due to these pre-existing database compatibility issues that weren't actually fixed.\n\nLet me document the findings: the acceptance criteria all check out, the local test run shows the expected behavior when DATABASE_URL isn't set, but once PostgreSQL is available in CI, the tests will hit failures and max out at 10 errors before stopping.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2804,"cache_read_input_tokens":61998,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_inpu